§ `s 83 Persons of pensionable age`

-- (2) "the relevant age" means pensionable age (within the meaning given by the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the
-- Pensions Act 1995) or, if higher, 65. The Pensions Act 1995 is not among the sources, so whether the person has reached
-- pensionable age is an input. A person has reached 65 on the day their age in years is 65 or more (fork F-83).
-- src:1438 | (2) In subsection (1) “the relevant age” means—
-- src:1439 | (a) pensionable age (within the meaning given by the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995); or
-- src:1440 | (b) if higher, 65.
@ref s 83(2) (src:1438-1440)
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the person has reached pensionable age within the meaning given by the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`s 83(2) the person has reached the relevant age` MEANS
    `the person has reached pensionable age within the meaning given by the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995`
    AND `the person`'s `age in years` AT LEAST 65

-- src:1437 | (1) A person is not entitled to the daily living component or the mobility component for any period after the person reaches the relevant age.
@ref s 83(1) (src:1437)
GIVEN `the person` IS A Person
      `the person has reached pensionable age within the meaning given by the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995` IS A BOOLEAN
      `regulations provide an exception to section 83(1) that covers the person` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 83(1) the person is not barred by having reached the relevant age` MEANS
    IF `s 83(2) the person has reached the relevant age` `the person` `the person has reached pensionable age within the meaning given by the rules in paragraph 1 of Schedule 4 to the Pensions Act 1995`
    THEN CONSIDER `regulations provide an exception to section 83(1) that covers the person`
         -- the exception lifts the bar: "Subsection (1) is subject to such exceptions as may be provided by regulations" (s 83(3))
         WHEN JUST TRUE  THEN JUST TRUE
         -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "A person is not entitled to the daily living component or the mobility component for any period after the person reaches the relevant age"
         WHEN JUST FALSE THEN JUST FALSE
         -- NOTHING-kind (points outside the text): the exceptions are left to regulations (section 83(3))
         WHEN NOTHING    THEN NOTHING
    ELSE JUST TRUE

§ `s 84 No entitlement to daily living component where UK is not competent state`

-- (2) The two relevant EU Regulations.
